{% extends 'guides/how_long_by_pieces/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% import 'guides/_macros.html.twig' as guide %}

{% block size_intro %}
    <p>
        2000 pieces is the biggest size in this series, and the median solve takes
        {{ guide.duration_words(distribution.medianSeconds) }} of active
        puzzling{% if distribution.medianSeconds > 8 * 3600 %} — more than a full working day at the table{% endif %}.
        Spread over a weekend or a string of evenings, the calendar time can be anything; the useful question is how
        many hours of actual puzzling a 2000-piece puzzle holds, and that is what we measured.
    </p>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_callout %}
    <div class="guide-callout">
        <p>
            Only a small group of puzzlers times their 2000-piece solves: {{ distribution.solvesCount|number_format }}
            solves by {{ distribution.playersCount|number_format }} puzzlers so far. The middle half of them lands
            anywhere between {{ distribution.p25Seconds|compactTime }} and {{ distribution.p75Seconds|compactTime }} —
            a range of {{ ((distribution.p75Seconds - distribution.p25Seconds) / 3600)|round }} hours, so treat the median
            as a rough centre rather than a promise.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_factors %}
    <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
            <strong>A table that fits it.</strong> A finished 2000-piece puzzle is usually around a metre wide and 70
            centimetres deep (about 38 × 27 in). If it does not fit on one table, a puzzle board or mat you can roll up
            between sessions saves re-sorting.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Working in sections.</strong> With 2000 pieces, building separate regions — a building, a tree line,
            the lettering — and joining them later often beats working outwards from one corner. Each section is a
            smaller puzzle with its own sorted pile.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Keeping the pieces sorted between sessions.</strong> Trays or boxes per colour group mean the next
            session starts building straight away instead of searching the whole pile again.
        </li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}
